Microbial compositions.

To identify the phylogenetic diversity of microbial communities in different soil
samples, qualified tags were assigned to phyla, classes, and genera. The phylum level identification of bacterial
and fugal communities is illustrated in Supplementary Fig. 2. In total, 28 and 4 identified phyla were observed,
respectively. For bacterial communities, a major difference in phylum level identification of bacterial communities between samples ABCD and E was the relative abundance of Chloroflexi, which played an important role in
carbon cycling33. This difference may be attributed to regional divergence and differences in DOC concentration.
It was found that the relative abundance of Bacteroidetes in samples C and D was higher than that in samples
A, B, and E, especially in sample C. There was a hypothesis that bacteria in Bacteroidetes belonged to MGPM.
To prove this hypothesis, community compositions at genus level should be detailedly summarized. Some isolates belonging to Actinobacteria, Gram-negative Proteobacteria or Gram-positive Firmicutes were MGPM29–31.
Proteobacteria, Firmicutes, and Actinobacteria were highly enriched in all samples. The total relative abundance
of these three phyla ranged from 19.2% (B02) to 97.2% (B04), only four samples of which were under 50%.
These results indicate that the presence of these microbes may be beneficial for the growth of mushrooms. For
