2.1 Conceptual Framework
The theoretical basis of this research is environmental
geochemistry, plant physiology and exposure pathway.
In the conceptualization of soil contamination, the
introduction of substances that are hazardous, namely
the heavy metals, the residues of the agrochemicals, the
hydrocarbons, and the industrial pollutants, into the
soils in levels that are beyond the levels that are found
in the natural geological backgrounds is considered as
soil contamination. Upon occurrence, the pollutants
combine with the physicochemical properties of soils,
affecting their bioavailability and movement. The
concepts incorporated in the framework include the
following:
1.Contaminant Input Sources:
It can have an anthropogenic (mining, fertilizers, waste
dumping, irrigation with polluted water) and natural
(geological weathering) pathways.
