INTRODUCTION
The presence and contamination of hazardous
metals in landfill leachates that end up in our
water resources have caused a great deal of
concern in recent years (Joseph et al., 2019).
Landfill leachates are complicated effluents with
a variable makeup that includes a large number of
biorecalcitrant and extremely harmful substances
(Scandelai et al., 2017). Heavy metals like Hg, Cd,
Pb, As, Cr, and phenol, which pose significant
risks to the environment and human health if
ignored, are found in landfill leachate (Hussein et
al., 2021). Chromium (Cr), cadmium (Cd), lead
(Pb), mercury (Hg), nickel (Ni), copper (Cu), zinc
(Zn), iron (Fe), and selenium (Se) are among the
metals frequently discovered in landfill leachate
(Kilgo et al., 2022). These pollutants are
commonly found in landfill leachate and, if not
treated, may pose a significant threat to human
health and the environment.
In terms of remediation, metals have been
removed from aqueous streams in the past using
electrochemical, adsorption, ion exchange, and
chemical precipitation techniques (Aijaz et al.,
2022; Razzak et al., 2022). These processes,
which are hampered by partial removal, require
the use of a large quantity of chemicals at a high
cost, particularly when pollutant concentrations
are in the 10 to 100 mg/L range (Razzak et al.,
2022). They are also time-consuming and produce
more trash that needs to be thrown away (Razzak
et al., 2022). The most effective and efficient way
to adsorb harmful metals and other contaminants
from the aquatic ecosystem is through adsorption.
The traditional methods for removing these
contaminants from landfill leachate are frequently

pricey, energy-intensive, and produce a large
amount of sludge that must be disposed of (Guo et
al., 2022). They also have low efficiency in
removing low concentrations of pollutants, high
energy and chemical consumption, process
complexity, and high maintenance and
operational costs (Tran et al., 2023). Numerous
studies have examined the potential of utilising
effective, affordable agricultural waste material as
an alternative to activated carbon for the
adsorption of harmful metals from wastewater
(Amalina et al., 2023).
Due to its ease of use and maintenance, broad
applicability, quick kinetics, lack of reaction
byproducts, environmental friendliness, and costeffectiveness for detoxification, the use of various
adsorbents to remove hazardous metals from
landfill leachate via adsorption has been proven to
be an effective method (Tang et al., 2019). See
also Deaconu and Senin (2016); Paunovic et al.
(2019). However, selecting a suitable adsorbent
has always been the most difficult step. In light of
this, adsorbents made from natural resources,
agricultural waste, and household and industrial
waste provide a less expensive way to remove
phenol and hazardous metals from wastewater.
Pineapple leftovers are a waste product that may
be used to remove pollutants from wastewater in
a cost-effective and environmentally friendly
way.
The effectiveness and behaviour of adsorption can
be influenced by interactions between the various
contaminants present in the liquid phase of
leachate, which is a complicated matrix (Pap et al.,
2021). Additionally, using waste biomass
(pineapple leftovers) as an adsorbent source
material can lower the treatment expenses. The

125 | This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License

East African Journal of Environment and Natural Resources, Volume 9, Issue 1, 2026
Article DOI: https://doi.org/10.37284/eajenr.9.1.4449

adsorption process utilises biochar as an adsorbent
material in the treatment of landfill leachate.
Samsudin et al. (2022) studied the use of biochar
in this process and found encouraging outcomes.
Because of its porous structure and big surface
area, biochar has a higher adsorption capacity for
heavy metals and organic molecules, making it a
great material for treating landfill leachate (Gul et
al., 2019). The biochar's surface functional groups
are crucial to the adsorption process, which
dictates its adsorption efficiency and selectivity
(Abbey et al., 2023).
Pineapple leaf biochar (PLB) eliminated a variety
of contaminants, demonstrating the potential
adsorption capabilities of biochar made from
pineapple plant components. Ni (II), Zn (II), and
Cu (II) were removed from aqueous solution via
mechanisms such as surface complexation, cation
exchange, and with maximum capacities of
around 44.9, 46.0, and 53.1 mg/g, respectively, as
well as the liberation of cations throughout the
process of adsorption (Iamsaard et al., 2022). The
biochar produced from pineapple peel also
demonstrates the potential to remove nutrients:
the pyrolysed pineapple peel biochar, which was
manufactured at 300 - 500 °C, was able to absorb
phosphate from aqueous solution, with the
amount of adsorption depending on the pyrolysis
temperature (Diaz et al., 2020). Pineapple biochar
can be made to perform better by changing it.
Pineapple residue biochar is a promising material
for the treatment of landfill leachate (Zhang et al.,
2018). Due to its surface functional groups and
porous structure, it has a high capacity for
adsorbing heavy metals and organic substances
(Ahmed et al., 2021). This biochar material may
be inferred to be a cost-effective and efficient
option for treating wastewater from leachate
(Shakoor et al., 2021). Factors such as pH, contact
time, temperature, adsorbate concentration, and
adsorbent dosage have a major impact on the
adsorption of toxic metals and phenol from
leachate by pineapple residue biochar. The high
surface area, big pore volume, and functional
groups with oxygen and nitrogen atoms in
pineapple residue biochar provide binding sites
for the removal of toxic metals and phenol from

the leachate, which contributes to its great
adsorption efficiency (Li et al., 2019).
Consequently, there is a need for a long-term,
effective method for removing pollutants from
landfill leachate. As a result, we had to look for
different, more affordable, and more effective
ways to treat wastewater.
The leachate from landfills in many developing
nations has high levels of organic contaminants,
such phenol as well as hazardous heavy metals
like nickel (Ni) and chromium (Cr). These
pollutants are a major risk to groundwater quality,
aquatic life, and human health. Current treatment
methods are frequently costly, technologically
complex, and unable to effectively remove
combined contaminants at the same time. The
majority of studies still concentrate on
conventional biomass feedstocks and singlecontaminant systems, even if biochar has proven
to be a potential low-cost adsorbent. It is unknown
whether pineapple residual biochar can
simultaneously absorb Cr, Ni, and phenol from
actual landfill leachate. Since pineapple residue is
a widely accessible waste that may be transformed
into a useful material, utilising pineapple residue
biochar as a leachate wastewater treatment
technique is a potential sustainable solution.
Owing to its high cellulose and lignin content,
which gives sufficient surface area for adsorption,
pineapple peel is an effective biochar adsorbent.
The active functional groups of pineapple residue
biochar can aid the adsorption of chromium,
nickel, and phenol from landfill leachate.
However, there has been little research on the
efficacy of pineapple residue biochar for the
removal of chromium, nickel, and phenol from
landfill leachate. Besides, pineapple residues are
still an undervalued waste and, when improperly
discarded, they become a public health and
environmental concern due to their ability to
contribute to the proliferation of pathogens. This
study was to evaluate the adsorption potential of
pineapple residue biochar for the removal of
chromium (Cr), nickel (Ni), and phenol from
landfill leachate.

