Keywords: tinplate, tin-iron alloy, pack test, electrochemical measurements, corrosion
2

1.

Introduction

2

The tinplate (tin-plated steel plate) used for food cans may have an unlacquered or

3

lacquered inner surface depending on the type of contents. In particular, when the contents

4

are (acidic) fruits, a tinplate with an unlacquered inner surface is often used. This is

5

because the oxygen remaining in the can is consumed by the oxidation of the tin,

6

preventing oxidative deterioration of the fruit's color, taste, aroma, and vitamins, and the

7

tin's sacrificial corrosion protection of iron prevents the can from developing holes.

8

The properties of tinplate itself and the contents of a can significantly impact corrosion

9

of the inner surface of the can. The effects on corrosion of the tin and tin-iron alloy

10

constituting the tinplate, as well as the ingredients of contents, have been investigated in

11

various ways, such as an analysis of corroded samples and electrochemical measurement

12

of tinplate in organic acid [1-16].

13

However, the can life of commercially available unlacquered tinplate cans for fruit use

14


