1    1.    Introduction
2    The tinplate (tin-plated steel plate) used for food cans may have an unlacquered or
3    lacquered inner surface depending on the type of contents. In particular, when the contents
4    are (acidic) fruits, a tinplate with an unlacquered inner surface is often used. This is
5    because the oxygen remaining in the can is consumed by the oxidation of the tin,
6    preventing oxidative deterioration of the fruit's color, taste, aroma, and vitamins, and the
7    tin's sacrificial corrosion protection of iron prevents the can from developing holes.
8    The properties of tinplate itself and the contents of a can significantly impact corrosion




