21   3.1     Accelerated pack test
22        Fig. 4 shows changes in the appearance of the can inner surface over time. By the third
23   month, spangles (tin crystal patterns) characteristic of the tinplate were visible all over
24   the surface. These are tin solidification structures formed during reflow (plating alloying
25   process) in the manufacture of tinplate, and they could be observed even before the
26   accelerated storage test. At 4 months, the area near the waterline of the can (i.e., the syrup
27   side of the interface between the headspace and syrup) had turned gray, and at 6 months,
28   the entire inner surface of the can (excluding the headspace) had turned gray. In addition,
29   as shown in Fig. 4, several lines appeared in the circumferential direction of the inner
30   surface of the can. As shown in the appearance of the can in Fig. 1, these were the beads

